<BaseLayout title="Worked Shopping Comparison Examples | Which Deal's Better?" description="Follow practical examples for package sizes, multipacks, mixed units, sales, coupons, and stock-up decisions using transparent calculations." allowAds>
<section class="content-section"><article class="shell prose"><p class="eyebrow">Put the formulas to work</p><h1>Worked shopping examples</h1><p>Each example starts with the package labels and ends with the decision information those numbers support. Prices are invented for explanation; replace them with the offers in front of you.</p>
<h2>Different package sizes: 12 oz versus 18 oz</h2><p>A 12 oz bag costs $3.49 and an 18 oz bag costs $4.79. Divide price by ounces: $3.49 ÷ 12 is about $0.291 per ounce; $4.79 ÷ 18 is about $0.266. The 18 oz bag is lower by roughly 2.5 cents per ounce.</p><p>At 18 ounces, the smaller bag’s rate would cost about $5.24, so the larger bag is roughly 45 cents cheaper for an equal amount. It still costs $1.30 more at checkout than one small bag. <a href="/">Compare these deals</a> when the shelf prices change.</p>
<h2>Multipacks with different contents</h2><p>Deal A is four 12 oz bottles for $10.80: 4 × 12 = 48 fl oz, or $0.225 per fl oz. Deal B is six 8 oz bottles for $11.52: also 48 fl oz, or $0.24 per fl oz. Comparing only four bottles with six would incorrectly favor the package with more containers. Here the total volume is equal and Deal A saves 72 cents.</p>
<h2>Mixed units: 16 oz versus 1.5 lb</h2><p>A 16 oz package is $4.64. A 1.5 lb package is $6.48. Convert 1.5 lb × 16 = 24 oz. The first costs $0.29 per ounce; the second costs $0.27 per ounce. The 1.5 lb package has the lower unit price, although it needs $1.84 more at checkout.</p>
<h2>A sale sticker versus a larger regular package</h2><p>A sale bag contains 10 oz for $3.20. A regular 24 oz bag costs $7.20. The sale bag is $0.32 per ounce; the regular larger bag is $0.30. The sale is real relative to its former price, but it is not the lowest unit-price option in this comparison.</p>
<h2>Percentage off versus dollars off</h2><p>On a $30 item, 20% off saves $6 and produces a $24 final price. A $5 coupon produces $25, so percentage off wins by $1. On a $20 item, 20% saves $4 and the $5 coupon wins by $1. The coupon headline cannot be compared without the eligible price. Try the <a href="/coupon-comparator/">Coupon & Discount Comparator</a> with sale prices and quantities.</p>
<h2>Usage history changes a stock-up decision</h2><p>Suppose 12 rolls lasted 42 days. The historical rate is 12 ÷ 42, about 0.286 rolls per day, or two rolls per week. Buying 36 rolls at the same rate represents roughly 126 days of supply.</p><p>If each 12-roll package is normally $15 and on sale for $12, three packages save $9 against today’s normal price. Each extra package adds about 42 estimated days and $3 of sale savings. That does not decide whether four months of supply fits your storage, budget, or product needs, but it makes the tradeoff concrete. Record your own periods in the <a href="/usage-tracker/">Usage Tracker</a>, then select that history in the <a href="/stock-up-calculator/">Stock-Up Calculator</a>.</p>
<h2>Use the right tool for the question</h2><p><a href="/">Compare Deals</a> answers which package has the lower normalized price. The coupon tool answers which promotion produces the lower final checkout total. Usage history supplies a personal rate, and the stock-up estimate combines that rate with sale economics. The <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> documents the exact rules behind all four.</p>
</article></section></BaseLayout>
